/// Lifecycle of an EvaluationScheme revision.
///
/// Deliberately a SEPARATE enum from GradeScaleStatus despite sharing values
/// today. The two aggregates version independently, and a regulation is the one
/// likely to gain a state a grading vocabulary never needs — a
/// PENDING_APPROVAL step for Controller-of-Examination sign-off. Sharing one
/// enum would force that state onto GradeScale as well.
enum EvaluationSchemeStatus {
  DRAFT
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
}

/// How a course sat more than once is reconciled when CGPA is computed.
///
/// BEST_ATTEMPT  — the highest grade point counts; the rest are history.
/// LATEST_ATTEMPT— the most recent attempt supersedes all earlier ones.
/// FIRST_ATTEMPT — the original attempt stands; re-sits clear a backlog but do
///                 not improve the grade.
/// ALL_ATTEMPTS  — every attempt enters the average, including failures.
enum AttemptPolicy {
  BEST_ATTEMPT
  LATEST_ATTEMPT
  FIRST_ATTEMPT
  ALL_ATTEMPTS
}

/// Rounding applied to a computed figure, named after the IEEE 754 / Java
/// BigDecimal vocabulary so the intent is unambiguous to any implementer.
///
/// HALF_UP   — 2.5 -> 3, -2.5 -> -3. The common institutional default.
/// HALF_DOWN — 2.5 -> 2.
/// HALF_EVEN — 2.5 -> 2, 3.5 -> 4. Banker's rounding; removes the systematic
///             upward bias HALF_UP introduces across a large cohort.
/// FLOOR     — always toward negative infinity.
/// CEILING   — always toward positive infinity.
enum RoundingMode {
  HALF_UP
  HALF_DOWN
  HALF_EVEN
  FLOOR
  CEILING
}

enum EvaluationComponentType {
  THEORY
  PRACTICAL
  VIVA
  LAB
  ASSIGNMENT
  QUIZ
  ATTENDANCE
  PROJECT
  SEMINAR
  PRESENTATION
  INTERNAL
  EXTERNAL
  CUSTOM
}

/// How ONE leaf component's repeated sessions reduce to a single figure.
///
/// SUM            — every session adds up; the scale is the total of them.
/// AVERAGE        — the mean of every session sat.
/// BEST_N         — the highest N sessions count; ruleConfig.count supplies N.
/// DROP_LOWEST_N  — the lowest N are discarded; ruleConfig.count supplies N.
/// MAX            — the single highest session.
/// LATEST         — the most recent session supersedes the rest.
///
/// Replaces the superseded EvaluationRuleType, which mixed these session-level
/// operators with the child-level ones now in ComponentRollup.
enum ComponentAggregation {
  SUM
  AVERAGE
  BEST_N
  DROP_LOWEST_N
  MAX
  LATEST
}

/// How a BRANCH component's children reduce to its own figure.
///
/// WEIGHTED_SUM — each child contributes its weightage percent. The ordinary
///                case, and the only one whose siblings must total 100.
/// SUM          — children add up on their raw scales, weightage ignored.
/// AVERAGE      — the unweighted mean of the children.
enum ComponentRollup {
  WEIGHTED_SUM
  SUM
  AVERAGE
}

/// What a recorded mark means.
///
/// RECORDED  — the student sat and was marked. marksObtained is present.
/// ABSENT    — the student did not sit. marksObtained is NULL, which is
///             different from a mark of zero: a mandatory component treats the
///             two differently, and a transcript shows them differently.
/// WITHHELD  — a mark exists but must not be used or shown, pending a
///             disciplinary or financial hold. marksObtained is present, since
///             a student who did not sit is ABSENT rather than withheld.
///
/// The invariant, enforced by the service because no column-level constraint
/// can express it: marksObtained is NULL exactly when the status is ABSENT.
enum MarkStatus {
  RECORDED
  ABSENT
  WITHHELD
}

/// The lifecycle of one assessment sitting.
///
/// DRAFT     — scheduled, but mark entry is not yet open. An assessment
///             calendar is built before any paper is marked, and a sitting in
///             this state accepts nothing.
/// OPEN      — mark entry is permitted. This is the ONLY state in which a mark
///             may be created or amended; that single rule is what locking
///             means.
/// LOCKED    — marks are frozen and verified, but not yet visible to students.
///             The verification gate: a sitting cannot go straight from OPEN to
///             PUBLISHED, so nothing reaches a student without someone having
///             closed entry first.
/// PUBLISHED — marks are visible to students.
///
/// Two transitions run BACKWARDS, deliberately. LOCKED -> OPEN reopens entry to
/// correct an error, and PUBLISHED -> LOCKED is the unpublish the workflow
/// requires. Both are audited like any other change. A lifecycle with no
/// correction path is the defect recorded as TD-C40, where an accidental
/// revocation could only be undone through direct database access.
enum AssessmentEventStatus {
  DRAFT
  OPEN
  LOCKED
  PUBLISHED
}

/// Why a course enrolment exists.
///
/// REGULAR         — the ordinary first sitting, within the student's own
///                   programme.
/// ELECTIVE        — chosen from the programme's own elective basket.
/// OPEN_ELECTIVE   — chosen from another department's offering. Distinct from
///                   ELECTIVE because degree audit counts the two against
///                   different requirement buckets.
/// AUDIT           — attended without credit. Carries no weight in SGPA, which
///                   the engine derives from this member rather than from a
///                   separate flag.
/// BACKLOG         — a re-sit after the previous attempt FAILED on marks.
/// IMPROVEMENT     — a re-sit after the previous attempt PASSED, to better the
///                   grade. Reconciled under the scheme's attemptPolicy.
/// REPEAT          — the course is taken again from scratch because the
///                   previous attempt could not be completed — detained,
///                   debarred or withdrawn. Distinct from BACKLOG because the
///                   earlier attempt produced no mark to improve on, and a
///                   transcript treats the two differently.
/// CREDIT_TRANSFER — credit recognised from another institution. Carries
///                   credits but expects no marks, so the calculation engine
///                   skips it rather than reporting it as unmarked.
///
/// There is deliberately no SUMMER member: a summer term is a property of the
/// Semester row, not of the enrolment, and encoding it here would be a second
/// place to ask when a course was taken.
enum RegistrationType {
  REGULAR
  ELECTIVE
  OPEN_ELECTIVE
  AUDIT
  BACKLOG
  IMPROVEMENT
  REPEAT
  CREDIT_TRANSFER
}

/// The lifecycle of an academic enrolment.
///
/// REGISTERED — provisionally enrolled, within the add/drop window.
/// CONFIRMED  — enrolment finalised; the student now counts toward assessment
///              and appears on every roster.
/// DROPPED    — left during add/drop. Leaves NO transcript record.
/// WITHDRAWN  — left after confirmation. DOES appear on the transcript, which
///              is precisely why it is a separate state from DROPPED rather
///              than a nuance of one.
/// COMPLETED  — the contract is discharged and a published result exists.
/// CANCELLED  — the registration was an administrative error. Retained rather
///              than deleted, so the correction itself stays auditable.
///
/// REGISTERED and CONFIRMED are the ACTIVE states; the other four are terminal.
/// A registration is never physically deleted — destroying one would destroy
/// the historical roster this model exists to preserve.
enum RegistrationStatus {
  REGISTERED
  CONFIRMED
  DROPPED
  WITHDRAWN
  COMPLETED
  CANCELLED
}

/// What a passing criterion measures, and therefore when it is evaluated.
///
/// COMPONENT_SCORE         — one component's computed value. Course-scoped;
///                           requires componentId. This is "Theory Pass",
///                           "Internal Pass", "Practical Pass" — any of them,
///                           with no per-component enum value, because the
///                           component is named by a foreign key rather than by
///                           a hard-coded vocabulary.
/// ATTENDANCE_PERCENT      — attendance in the course. Course-scoped.
/// SEMESTER_CREDITS_EARNED — credits earned across the semester.
///                           SEMESTER-scoped: evaluated once per student per
///                           semester against their governing regulation, not
///                           once per course.
///
/// There is deliberately NO COURSE_TOTAL member. The overall pass mark is
/// already the boundary of the lowest passing GradeBand, and declaring it twice
/// would create two authorities for one question — the exact duplication this
/// schema forbids. See the note on PassingCriterion.
enum PassingMetric {
  COMPONENT_SCORE
  ATTENDANCE_PERCENT
  SEMESTER_CREDITS_EARNED
}

/// How a criterion's threshold should be read.
///
/// MARKS   — an absolute figure on the component's own scale, "21 out of 70".
/// PERCENT — a proportion, "30% of the component" or "75% attendance".
/// CREDITS — a credit count, for SEMESTER_CREDITS_EARNED.
enum ThresholdUnit {
  MARKS
  PERCENT
  CREDITS
}

/// The consequence of missing a threshold.
///
/// FAIL       — the requirement was not met. The student carries a backlog and
///              may clear it by re-sitting.
/// INELIGIBLE — the student was barred from the assessment, or from
///              progressing. Not a mark-based failure and not clearable by
///              re-sitting the same attempt.
///
/// Two values because the downstream consequence genuinely differs; no third
/// value for promotion, because at semester scope INELIGIBLE already reads as
/// "not eligible to progress".
enum CriterionOutcome {
  FAIL
  INELIGIBLE
}

/// The stage of the calculation pipeline a rule runs at.
///
/// The pipeline is: raw sessions → [SESSION_ADJUSTMENT] → component
/// aggregation (owned by EvaluationComponent) → [COMPONENT_ADJUSTMENT] →
/// weighted rollup up the tree → course total → [COURSE_ADJUSTMENT] → grade
/// assignment (owned by GradeScale).
///
/// SESSION_ADJUSTMENT   — transforms each raw session mark BEFORE the component
///                        aggregates them. "Two bonus marks on every quiz."
/// COMPONENT_ADJUSTMENT — transforms one component's aggregated figure.
///                        "Normalise the 20-mark internal onto 30."
/// COURSE_ADJUSTMENT    — transforms the course total after everything has
///                        rolled up. Grace, moderation and curving live here.
///
/// A new phase is a new pipeline stage, which needs engine code regardless, so
/// nothing is lost by making this an enum — and a great deal is gained, since
/// the engine dispatches on it and an unconstrained string would let a typo
/// silently disable a rule.
enum RulePhase {
  SESSION_ADJUSTMENT
  COMPONENT_ADJUSTMENT
  COURSE_ADJUSTMENT
}

/// What a rule does to the value flowing through its stage.
///
/// Every operation is a pure numeric transform: it takes the incoming value
/// (and, for cohort operations, the cohort's distribution) and returns one
/// number. None of them reads a pass threshold, assigns a grade, or alters the
/// component tree.
///
/// ADD_CONSTANT   — value + amount. Fixed bonus marks.
/// ADD_PERCENTAGE — value + value × percent/100. Proportional bonus.
/// SCALE          — value × factor. Normalising a component onto another scale.
/// CAP            — min(value, limit). The guard that stops a bonus exceeding
///                  the maximum; always the last rule in a stage.
/// FLOOR          — max(value, limit).
/// GRACE          — a conditional top-up of at most config.maxAward. The one
///                  operation that consults a threshold, and it does not own
///                  that threshold: PassingCriteria supplies it and the engine
///                  passes it in.
/// MODERATION     — shifts the cohort onto a target mean and spread.
/// CURVE          — redistributes the cohort onto a target distribution.
/// CUSTOM_FORMULA — evaluates a restricted, whitelisted expression tree. This
///                  is the escape hatch that makes "no redesign for a future
///                  rule" true: arithmetic nobody anticipated is a config
///                  value, not a migration.
///
/// MODERATION and CURVE are COHORT-SCOPED — they cannot be computed for one
/// student in isolation. That is derivable from the operation itself, so no
/// column records it; storing a flag would be a second source of truth for a
/// fact this enum already determines.
enum RuleOperation {
  ADD_CONSTANT
  ADD_PERCENTAGE
  SCALE
  CAP
  FLOOR
  GRACE
  MODERATION
  CURVE
  CUSTOM_FORMULA
}

/// Where a component's marks originate.
///
/// MANUAL_ENTRY       — typed in by faculty against an assessment session.
/// ATTENDANCE_DERIVED — computed from the student's attendance percentage via
///                      ruleConfig.attendanceBands. This is what makes
///                      "Attendance Marks" configuration rather than code.
/// ASSIGNMENT_DERIVED — pulled from the existing assignment submissions.
/// COMPUTED           — produced by the engine from other components; carries
///                      no marks of its own.
enum ComponentSource {
  MANUAL_ENTRY
  ATTENDANCE_DERIVED
  ASSIGNMENT_DERIVED
  COMPUTED
}

/// Lifecycle of a GradeScale revision.
///
/// DRAFT    — under construction, freely editable, never used by any
///            calculation. Bands may be incomplete; the tiling rule is checked
///            at activation, not on every insert, so a scale can be built up
///            one band at a time.
/// ACTIVE   — frozen and in use. Exactly one revision per (tenantId, code) may
///            hold this state; that single-active rule is enforced by the
///            activation transaction in the service layer, since PostgreSQL
///            cannot express it without a partial unique index that Prisma
///            would not track.
/// ARCHIVED — frozen and retired. Still readable forever, because results
///            already computed against it must remain reproducible.
enum GradeScaleStatus {
  DRAFT
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
}

enum GradeCalculationMethod {
  ABSOLUTE
  RELATIVE
}

enum ResultPublicationStatus {
  DRAFT
  VERIFIED
  APPROVED
  PUBLISHED
}

enum TranscriptStatus {
  GENERATED
  VERIFIED
  ISSUED
}

/// Phase 20 — Faculty Feedback System.
///
/// A versioned, tenant-owned QUESTIONNAIRE: the set of questions a student is
/// asked about one faculty member in one course.
///
/// NO FIXED RATING COLUMNS. "Teaching", "Behaviour", "Communication",
/// "Practical Knowledge" and "Infrastructure" are not columns anywhere in this
/// section — they are FeedbackCategory values a tenant writes its own questions
/// against. A university adding a ninth dimension configures a row rather than
/// requesting a migration, which is the entire point of the model.
///
/// OWNERSHIP    : Tenant, carried as a plain column. Unlike GradeScale, there
///                is no Tenant relation: a form is configuration, not a
///                governed regulation, and the FK would only add a cascade the
///                repository's tenant predicate already covers.
/// LIFECYCLE    : DRAFT -> OPEN -> CLOSED. Questions are editable only while
///                DRAFT; OPEN is the only state in which a submission may be
///                written; CLOSED freezes the cohort so a report is
///                reproducible.
/// VERSIONING   : (tenantId, code) names the form; `version` names the
///                revision. A submission pins one revision, so a report can
///                always state which questions were actually asked.
model FeedbackForm {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  tenantId String

  /// Stable identifier ACROSS versions, e.g. "LEC-FEEDBACK". Two rows sharing
  /// a code are two revisions of one questionnaire.
  code String

  name        String
  description String?

  /// Revision number within (tenantId, code), starting at 1. Never reused.
  version Int @default(1)

  status FeedbackFormStatus @default(DRAFT)

  /// Which kind of session this form is asked about. Reuses the existing
  /// SessionType rather than declaring a parallel enum: a lab feedback form and
  /// a lecture feedback form differ in the sessions they apply to, and that
  /// vocabulary already exists on Timetable and Attendance.
  sessionType SessionType @default(LECTURE)

  createdById String?

  /// When the status last changed. ONE column rather than a nullable timestamp
  /// per state, matching CourseRegistration and AssessmentEvent: AuditLog
  /// already holds every transition with its actor.
  statusChangedAt DateTime @default(now())

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// SetNull because DELETE /api/users/[id] is a hard delete that a Restrict
  /// here would begin rejecting. The permanent, actor-preserving record is
  /// AuditLog.
  createdBy User? @relation(fields: [createdById], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull, onUpdate: Cascade)

  questions   FeedbackQuestion[]
  submissions FeedbackSubmission[]

  /// The catalogue read: which forms are open, for this kind of session, in
  /// this tenant.
  @@index([tenantId, status, sessionType])
  /// One revision number per named form per tenant.
  @@unique([tenantId, code, version])
  /// Not a uniqueness rule — `id` is already unique. This is the TARGET of the
  /// composite foreign keys on FeedbackQuestion and FeedbackSubmission, which
  /// is what makes a cross-tenant child structurally impossible rather than
  /// merely avoided.
  @@unique([tenantId, id])
}

/// Phase 20 — one question on a form.
///
/// This model IS the "no fixed rating columns" requirement. The question text
/// is the tenant's, and `category` places it in a broad, institution-neutral
/// grouping that a report rolls up by.
///
/// OWNERSHIP    : FeedbackForm. A question has no lifecycle of its own and is
///                deleted with its form.
/// LIFECYCLE    : Mutable only while the owning form is DRAFT.
model FeedbackQuestion {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())

  /// Carried so the composite foreign key below can prove this question and its
  /// form belong to the same tenant. Constrained by that key to equal the
  /// form's tenantId, so the two cannot diverge.
  tenantId String
  formId   String

  /// Stable handle within the form — "Q1", "CLARITY". A report years later must
  /// name which question a score came from, and a cuid explains nothing.
  code String

  text String

  category FeedbackCategory

  /// How much this question counts in its category's average. Decimal(5,2)
  /// matching the weightage columns of Phase 16, and exact base-10 so a
  /// weighted mean has no binary rounding error. Defaults to 1.00, which is the
  /// unweighted case every tenant starts from.
  weight Decimal @default(1.00) @db.Decimal(5, 2)

  /// Presentation order within the form.
  sequence Int

  /// Whether an answer is required for the submission to be completable. The
  /// service branches on it, so it is a column.
  isRequired Boolean @default(true)

  /// Whether a free-text comment may accompany the rating. A question may be
  /// rating-only; this is what says so.
  allowsComment Boolean @default(false)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// Owning form, tenant-proving. Cascade because a question cannot outlive its
  /// form.
  form FeedbackForm @relation(fields: [tenantId, formId], references: [tenantId, id], onDelete: Cascade, onUpdate: Cascade)

  answers FeedbackAnswer[]

  /// THE hot read: one form's questions, in order.
  @@index([tenantId, formId, sequence])
  /// One handle per form. Both columns are NOT NULL, so PostgreSQL enforces
  /// this completely.
  @@unique([formId, code])
}

/// Phase 20 — one student's feedback on one faculty member, for one course, in
/// one semester, against one form.
///
/// ANONYMITY IS A PROJECTION, NOT A COLUMN.
///   studentId is stored, because a duplicate submission cannot otherwise be
///   refused and an audit cannot otherwise be answered. What keeps it away from
///   faculty is the repository publishing two projections and no third —
///   ANONYMOUS_SUBMISSION_SELECT omits the column entirely, so a faculty-facing
///   query physically cannot return an identity. There is no masking step to
///   forget.
///
/// LIFECYCLE    : DRAFT -> SUBMITTED. A DRAFT is a student mid-thought and is
///                never read by an analytic; only SUBMITTED rows are averaged.
model FeedbackSubmission {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  tenantId String

  formId String

  studentId String

  /// Who the feedback is ABOUT.
  facultyId String

  /// The course and term it concerns. Both required: feedback about a faculty
  /// member detached from what and when they taught cannot be reported on.
  courseId   String
  semesterId String

  status FeedbackSubmissionStatus @default(DRAFT)

  /// When the student finalised it. NULL exactly while the status is DRAFT.
  submittedAt DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// Owning form, tenant-proving. Cascade because a submission against a
  /// deleted form has no questions to interpret it.
  form FeedbackForm @relation(fields: [tenantId, formId], references: [tenantId, id], onDelete: Cascade, onUpdate: Cascade)

  /// Plain single-column keys: none of these pre-existing parents carries
  /// @@unique([tenantId, id]), and adding one would mean a blocking index build
  /// on a live, populated table. Cross-tenant consistency here is enforced by
  /// the repository, which filters every query on tenantId — a stated
  /// weakening, not an oversight.
  ///
  /// Cascade on Student, matching StudentDocument and OpenElectivePreference: a
  /// removed student's feedback has no author. NoAction on the other three,
  /// checked at end-of-statement, so a stray delete that would orphan a
  /// submission is refused while a tenant-level cascade still succeeds.
  student  Student       @relation(fields: [studentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade, onUpdate: Cascade)
  faculty  FacultyMember @relation(fields: [facultyId], references: [id], onDelete: NoAction, onUpdate: NoAction)
  course   Course        @relation(fields: [courseId], references: [id], onDelete: NoAction, onUpdate: NoAction)
  semester Semester      @relation(fields: [semesterId], references: [id], onDelete: NoAction, onUpdate: NoAction)

  answers FeedbackAnswer[]

  /// THE faculty report, exactly: every submission about one faculty member for
  /// one course in one term, filtered to SUBMITTED.
  @@index([tenantId, facultyId, courseId, semesterId, status])
  /// The per-form read: response counts and completion for one questionnaire.
  @@index([tenantId, formId, status])
  /// Supports the cascade on student deletion. PostgreSQL does not index a
  /// foreign key automatically.
  @@index([studentId])
  /// One submission per student, per faculty member, per course, per term, per
  /// form. This constraint — not a prior read — is what actually prevents a
  /// duplicate: two concurrent submits both see nothing and both proceed. Every
  /// column is NOT NULL, so PostgreSQL enforces it completely.
  @@unique([studentId, facultyId, courseId, semesterId, formId])
  /// Composite-FK target for FeedbackAnswer.
  @@unique([tenantId, id])
}

/// Phase 20 — one student's answer to one question.
///
/// OWNERSHIP    : FeedbackSubmission. An answer has no meaning without the
///                submission it belongs to and is deleted with it.
/// LIFECYCLE    : Replaced wholesale rather than diffed — the repository
///                deletes and re-inserts inside one transaction, because the
///                (submissionId, questionId) constraint makes a partial update
///                a minefield.
model FeedbackAnswer {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())

  /// Carried so the composite foreign key below can prove this answer and its
  /// submission belong to the same tenant, and so a cohort's answers can be
  /// read tenant-scoped in ONE statement without a join.
  tenantId     String
  submissionId String

  questionId String

  /// The rating, an integer on the 1..5 scale. SmallInt rather than Int because
  /// this is the largest table in the phase — students × faculty × courses ×
  /// questions — and the range is bounded by two orders of magnitude more than
  /// it will ever need. The bounds themselves are enforced by Zod at the
  /// service boundary, where a violation can be reported to the student.
  rating Int @db.SmallInt

  /// Free text, permitted only when the question sets allowsComment.
  comment String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// Owning submission, tenant-proving. Cascade because an answer cannot
  /// outlive its submission.
  submission FeedbackSubmission @relation(fields: [tenantId, submissionId], references: [tenantId, id], onDelete: Cascade, onUpdate: Cascade)

  /// The question answered. Cascade because an answer to a deleted question
  /// cannot be interpreted, and questions are only deletable while the form is
  /// DRAFT — a state in which no submission exists.
  question FeedbackQuestion @relation(fields: [questionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade, onUpdate: Cascade)

  /// One submission's answers, and the cohort read that aggregates a report
  /// without an N+1.
  @@index([tenantId, submissionId])
  /// Supports the cascade on question deletion.
  @@index([questionId])
  /// A student cannot answer one question twice. Both columns are NOT NULL, so
  /// PostgreSQL enforces this completely.
  @@unique([submissionId, questionId])
}

/// Lifecycle of a feedback form revision.
///
/// DRAFT  — being written. Invisible to students; questions may still change.
/// OPEN   — students may submit and edit. The ONLY state in which a submission
///          may be written.
/// CLOSED — submissions frozen. The cohort is fixed, which is what makes a
///          report reproducible.
///
/// Deliberately a SEPARATE enum from OpenElectiveStatus and
/// EvaluationSchemeStatus despite the shared shape: the three aggregates
/// version independently, and a form is the one likely to gain a state the
/// others never need.
enum FeedbackFormStatus {
  DRAFT
  OPEN
  CLOSED
}

/// The broad dimension a question measures.
///
/// This enum is what replaces fixed rating columns. It is institution-neutral
/// on purpose — nothing here names a department, a course or a teaching style —
/// because these must mean the same thing to every tenant for a cross-faculty
/// report to be comparable. A tenant needing finer granularity writes more
/// QUESTIONS in a category, which is a row, not a migration. OVERALL carries
/// the summary question every form ends on.
enum FeedbackCategory {
  TEACHING
  BEHAVIOUR
  COMMUNICATION
  PRACTICAL_KNOWLEDGE
  INFRASTRUCTURE
  LAB_EXPERIENCE
  OVERALL
}

/// Lifecycle of one student's submission.
///
/// DRAFT     — saved but not finalised. A student mid-thought. NEVER read by an
///             analytic: averaging a half-finished form would let it move a
///             faculty member's score when the student never said it was done.
/// SUBMITTED — finalised. The only status any statistic reads.
///
/// There is deliberately no WITHDRAWN member: a student who changes their mind
/// while the form is OPEN edits their answers, and once the form is CLOSED the
/// cohort is fixed by design.
enum FeedbackSubmissionStatus {
  DRAFT
  SUBMITTED
}
